    type='think',
    think="But wait, the issue is that when pending doesn't match, I still use 
playPassage which plays to terminal. That's fine if the crossing only has one s
ignature or if the desired key comes after. But if the first pending is non-mat
ching and the crossing has multiple signatures, playPassage will resolve the fi
rst and then continue to terminal, possibly hitting the desired key. Actually p
layPassage DOES resolve choices, so if the desired key is after the non-matchin
g one, playPassage will resolve it too. So using playPassage to resolve non-mat
ching is OK. The problem was only the initial detection of pending.\n\nActually
the real problem was the `else` branch: when no pending, I called playPassage w
hich consumes the upcoming pending internally and never returns pending to the 
outer loop. By changing to `passageAct(state, survive(state))`, the outer loop 
will catch pending in the next iteration.\n\nBut when pending exists and doesn'
t match, playPassage resolves it and returns terminal or non-pending. That's OK
for finding a later matching key? No, because playPassage might play to termina
l and never give the outer loop a chance to check for the desired key after res
olving the non-matching one. But playPassage internally will hit any subsequent
signatures and resolve them. It returns after terminal or no-op. So if the desi
red key is after the non-matching one, playPassage will consume it too, and the
outer loop won't see it.\n\nHowever, for the determinism test, I can scan for a
crossing where the FIRST pending is the desired key. The `if (state.pending)` c
heck catches it immediately. I only fall into the non-matching path if the firs
t pending is a different key. Since I scan in fixed order, I'll find a crossing
where the first pending matches eventually.\n\nActually, I could improve by ste
pping more carefully, but it's not necessary. The scan will find a matching fir
st-pending crossing.\n\nBut there's still a subtle issue: the first `passageAct
` may set pending. Then the next iteration sees it and returns. Good.\n\nLet me
re-run the tests.",
    encrypted=None
)
